Long-context behaviour (measured)
---------------------------------
  Everything above used an almost-empty context. These run against one
  already-running server, so they cost seconds rather than another startup.

    ready in 16s
    history     23 tok: prefill    16.9 tok/s (  1.4s to first token), decode 10.15 tok/s
    history   1887 tok: prefill    59.0 tok/s ( 32.0s to first token), decode 10.60 tok/s
    history   7509 tok: prefill    62.7 tok/s (119.7s to first token), decode 10.83 tok/s

| history | prefill | time to first token | decode |
|---|---|---|---|
| 23 tok | 17 tok/s | 1.4 s | 10.15 tok/s |
| 1887 tok | 59 tok/s | 32.0 s | 10.60 tok/s |
| 7509 tok | 63 tok/s | 119.7 s | 10.83 tok/s |

Decode falls 0% between an empty context and 7509 tokens of history.

Same config at --ctx-size 32768 (measured)
------------------------------------------
  A longer context costs VRAM for the KV cache, which can force more experts
  back onto the CPU. This measures whether it does.

    ready in 16s
    rep0: 9.89 tok/s
    rep1: 9.90 tok/s
    rep2: 9.87 tok/s
  9.89 tok/s (spread 0.3%), 20826 MiB peak VRAM  -> -5.2% vs ctx 16384
  For reference the cost model predicted 5.17 tok/s at --n-cpu-moe 43; measured
  is +102% against that. The measured number is the one to trust.

  Bandwidth-bound: 96% of each token is spent reading experts from system RAM at
  23 GB/s. Thread count and most other flags will not move this.

  The only large wins available are: more VRAM (offload more layers), faster
  RAM, or a smaller quantisation.

  Measured 10.43 tok/s is 202% of the 5.17 tok/s roofline. There is essentially
  nothing left to tune -- stop here.

